TODO
====

Grouped by kind rather than priority. Line references were checked against the
tree; if one has drifted, the description should still find it.


Packaging
---------

The port is done: all three platforms build, are real CI gates, and have been
run and heard on real hardware. One thing it never covered.

- The macOS .app has never been run on a Mac without GTK on it -- which is
  every ordinary Mac, and is what bundling GTK into it is for. CI cannot check
  this: building at all means Homebrew put GTK on the runner, so a bundle that
  resolved against it looks like one carrying its own. macOS does run, but that
  was built from source against the GTK it needed to build in the first place,
  which is a different claim.

  Windows is done -- the zip has been unpacked and run on a machine that has
  never had GTK. Homebrew's keg symlinks and the two-prefix problem are macOS
  only, so that does not carry over.

  Does not apply to Linux, where the package is meant to use the
  distribution's GTK.  (docs/PACKAGING.md)


Never heard
-----------

- No MIDI has ever been played through the RtMidi path, on any platform. The
  headless checks pass; that is not the same thing.

- Latency, underrun behaviour under load, and whether RtAudio's JACK path is as
  good as the hand-written one are all open and want an ear. JACK users are the
  audience that will notice.


The corpus
----------

- 11 of the 92 shipped DSPs do not load at all. They reference input/wav,
  input/alsa or misc/wlan -- plugins that compile but are deliberately not in
  the build, wav.cpp's own description string being "Wav Input (BROKEN)".
  Remove or fix (argify) the obsolete ones. All 101 patches load, so the
  patch gate no longer filters anything.

- Four DSPs have numerically unstable filters and are being saved by the
  limiter rather than fixed: old/test.dsp diverges to 1.75e5, old/bd10.dsp to
  2e4. They want looking at separately.  (docs/AUDIO.md)


Coverage
--------

- dspcheck should grow a "load this and process it" check for anything the
  editor writes.

- A dspstress TSan job is deliberately out of the default matrix: TSan cannot
  be combined with ASan so it needs its own build tree, and the run is slow.
  Worth a nightly schedule: trigger once the rest is stable.

- thSynthTree::buildSynthTree walks the graph recursively with a recalc flag
  rather than a proper topological order. An editor makes it easy to build
  pathological graphs.

- Nothing gated compares rendered audio against a reference. dspcheck asserts
  a render is deterministic and dsplevel asserts it is inside the rails;
  neither notices it becoming a *different* sound. Found by accident: making
  every channel of the mix read out0 passed all thirteen ctest gates, and a
  bitwise dump of the corpus caught it in one line. dspab does this across two
  sets of plugins, and cannot do it across two builds of libthink, which is
  where a change to the mix loop lives. That wants either a dspab that takes
  two library paths or a small dump-and-cmp harness.


Engine
------

- RT-safety, what is left of it. The per-window path is clear: thMidiChan no
  longer builds an std::string per channel per note per window, no longer
  searches a std::map for it, and no longer lets thSynthTree::getArg invent
  and insert the arg it could not find; the two VLAs are buffers owned by the
  channel. Audio is bit-identical across the corpus. Still outstanding, and
  none of it per window:

    * A note's args are resized from 1 to windowlen on its first window, so
      every plugin holding state allocates once per note, on the audio
      thread. Sizing them when the note is built would move that to the GUI
      thread, where the rest of a note's allocation already happens.
    * insertNote and releaseNote reach `trigger' by name. Per key press.
    * buildSynthTree's recursive recalc walk, above.  (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md)

- Slider values can be torn-read. Deliberate -- routing every drag through the
  queue would be silly -- but the honest fix is an atomic or a smoothed
  parameter.

- Arg descriptions cover 33 of 314 args. That is every one an author had
  already written a comment about, and the rest are deliberately empty rather
  than invented -- but somebody who knows these DSPs could write the other
  281, and a tooltip is the cheapest documentation this tree has.  Likewise
  the eight declared defaults are the eight the plugins special-case zero
  for; others may have a sensible default nobody has written down.
  (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md)

- A @chanarg cannot be driven by a node. "This parameter varies with an LFO"
  works today; "the knob moves on its own" needs engine work.


Composition
-----------

- Fitness from the sound itself. `gen::breed' and `gen::evolve' both judge a
  candidate by arithmetic over the genome -- distance from a target vector,
  distance from the last champion, how much of the corridor got used. What
  neither can do is listen: judging the rendered audio needs a shadow synth
  to render candidates into, off the audio thread and without sounding, and
  a feature or two pulled out of the result. `thSynth::setSilent' is most of
  the machinery already, since the mirror needed the same thing. For `breed'
  this is one function replaced and nothing else moved, which is most of the
  argument for having built it the way it is.

  It is the last step of the composer plan nobody has taken. Everything
  above it landed: presets, the wildcard sink, morph, the GA over chanarg
  vectors, the structure edits, `composer_input' and `composer_capture'.

- A composer that changes the *shape* of an instrument's graph, not just a
  number in it. `gen::swap' replaces a channel's whole graph by name and
  `gen::reshape' moves one constant inside the graph that is there; what
  neither can do is add a node, remove one, or rewire what feeds what.
  `THC_EV_NODEARG' refuses an arg that is wired to anything -- another node's
  output, a @chanarg, a note property -- precisely because writing a number
  over a wire is an add/remove/rewire edit wearing a value edit's clothes,
  and that is the refusal this would have to replace with a mechanism.

  What it wants is a third structure edit beside `THC_EV_PATCH' and
  `THC_EV_NODEARG', delivered the way those are: built on the GUI thread,
  swapped through the command queue, scheduled and sparse and drawn on the
  roll, so a composer still cannot thrash a graph faster than its event
  stream flows. The legal moves are already written down --
  `NodeGraph::canConnect' is the constraint function, so a mutated graph can
  be well-formed by construction rather than by luck -- and `loadTree' has
  been a live atomic instrument swap since before any of this existed.
  `thinklang' is pure, so candidate graphs parse off to one side without
  touching the live synth, and `NodeEdit' is the one-writer splice seam a
  program edits text through.

  Past that is the graph as a genome: `breed' and `evolve' over topologies
  rather than over chanarg vectors, which needs a graph that serializes and
  the audio-feature fitness above to judge what comes out. Research rather
  than a next step, and the reason to keep the refusal honest in the
  meantime.  (docs/UNIFICATION.md, "structural mutation")

- Meta chains: a chain that schedules or chooses among the sections rather
  than emitting notes, so the form of a piece is composed the way its notes
  are. `section' landed and is the data half of this; what is missing is
  something that reads it. The other road -- meta-composers splicing the
  piece file itself through `thcGenEdit', so a piece rewrites itself and Save
  publishes what it became -- verifies just as well and reads a great deal
  stranger. Data first.

- Writing a graph out *inline* in a `.gen', rather than naming a `.dsp' by
  file. An `instrument' block carries a filename and the chanarg values that
  make it this instrument, which delivered the self-contained piece; putting
  the graph itself in the same file needs the two grammars merged, and the
  merge is still waiting on a payoff rather than blocking one.
  (docs/UNIFICATION.md)


Node editor
-----------

- Renaming a control. Right-click gives its range, label and group; the name is
  shown and insensitive, because renaming has to rewrite every `in1 = @old' in
  the file as well as the block. That is removeControl plus addControl plus a
  rename of every reference, and it wants deciding on rather than bolting to a
  dialog about ranges.

- Nothing edits a .dsp's header. `name', `author', `description' and now
  `category' can only be written by hand or by NodeEdit::createFile, which
  writes the first two once and never again -- so the editor can rewire a
  graph it cannot retitle, and a graph saved under a new name goes on
  announcing the old one in every chooser. The Composer has this for a piece
  already and is the model: thcGenEdit::setInfo is generic over the key and
  splices the statement in, erases it when the box is emptied, and inserts it
  after the existing header lines when it is not there; ComposerWindow's Edit
  -> Piece tab is four entry boxes over it. What is wanted here is the same
  pair -- a NodeEdit::setInfo over the .dsp index, and somewhere in the node
  editor to type into.

  The category makes it worth doing rather than merely tidy. It is the one
  header field a chooser sorts on, so a graph somebody writes in the editor
  lands under Uncategorized with no way to move it that does not involve a
  text editor.  (src/NodeEdit.h, src/thcGenEdit.cpp setInfo,
  docs/DSP_FORMAT.md "category")


The browser page
----------------

- The page cannot ask what is on a channel. It knows what it put there
  itself, which is not the same question: a piece's own instruments, and
  whatever an earlier mode left, are invisible to it. Two things want the
  answer. The channels row says "as it is" over a channel nothing is loaded
  on, where it should say "nothing" -- and in piece mode, keys aimed at a
  channel the piece neither names nor listens on are silent with nothing in
  the page to say why. A `tw_loaded(channel)' export beside tw_sink_channel
  would settle both, and would let the key channel take a default too.
  (wasm/web/main.js)

- Four measurements the browser build has only ever been given on a fast
  desktop. The scheduler runs in the worklet on numbers taken there, and
  each of these could move that decision:

    * The worst quantum on a slow machine: the busiest pieces (`orrery',
      `tide') plus a chord of note-ons, at 256 and at 128.
      `wasm/web/bench.mjs' is the harness; what is missing is the machine.
    * Chrome's and Firefox's main-thread-to-worklet delay on each OS, with
      a real output device rather than an offline context.
    * Background-tab behavior through a silent stretch of a piece, in both
      browsers.
    * Whether a dedicated worker's timers really are unthrottled in the
      browsers the room targets. Secondary sources only, so far.
